			The below shows that $\beta_1,\ldots,\beta_p$ is an {\rm NSS} with a lower triangular {\rm NSM} of $W$. For any given $\beta_j$, let $\beta_j=\alpha'_{k,i}$. First, since $\phi(E_{\beta_j})\subset W_{J_k}$ and $W_{J_k}\cap{\it Cus}_l=\varnothing$ for each $l\in[q]$, we have $E_{\beta_j}\cap\phi^{-1}({\it Cus}_l)=\varnothing$ for each $l\in[q]$. Next, by \eqref{56eq1} we have $E_{\beta_j}\cap \phi^{-1}(C_i^{J_k})\neq\varnothing$, by \eqref{56eq3} we have $\psi(C_i^{J_k})={\it Ncus}_j$, thus $E_{\beta_j}\cap\phi^{-1}({\it Ncus}_j)\neq\varnothing$. Suppose $E_{\beta_j}\cap\phi^{-1}({\it Ncus}_h)\neq\varnothing$. By \eqref{56eq1}, we obtain ${\it Ncus}_h=\psi(C_{i'}^{J_k})$ for some $i'\leq i$. Let $\beta_h=\alpha'_{\tilde{k},\tilde{i}}$, then $(\tilde{k},\tilde{i})\leq_{\rm lex}(k,i')\leq_{\rm lex}(k,i)$ by \eqref{56eq3}, and then $h\leq j$ by \eqref{56eq2}. Thus, for each $h>j$, $E_{\beta_j}\cap\phi^{-1}({\it Ncus}_h)=\varnothing$. Therefore $\beta_1,\ldots,\beta_p$ is an {\rm NSS} with a lower triangular {\rm NSM} of $W$.

\label{cuslemma}
